**No, the rate limit will not block your uptime monitor.**

Here's the config in question (from [`Shopify/skai-train` → `projects/drawmatic/DEPLOYMENT.md`](https://github.com/Shopify/skai-train/blob/main/projects/drawmatic/DEPLOYMENT.md)):

```nginx
# Rate limiting
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=api:10m rate=10r/s;

# ...

location /api/ {
    limit_req zone=api burst=20 nodelay;   # ← rate limit only here
    proxy_pass http://nextjs;
}

location /health {
    access_log off;
    return 200 "healthy\n";                # ← no limit_req directive
}
```

Two independent reasons it's safe:

1. **`/health` is a separate `location` block with no `limit_req`.** nginx applies rate limiting only within the location where `limit_req` is explicitly declared. Requests to `/health` match the `location /health` block, never enter the `location /api/` block, and are never counted against the `api` zone.

2. **Even if `/health` were rate-limited, 1 request per 10 seconds (0.1 r/s) is far below the 10 r/s threshold** with burst=20. It wouldn't trigger a 429 regardless.

The same pattern holds in the other nginx configs in the search results ([`brisk`](https://github.com/Shopify/brisk/blob/main/router/brisk.conf), [`quick`](https://github.com/Shopify/skai-train/blob/main/projects/quick/router/quick.conf)) — `/health` is always a standalone location with no `limit_req`, while rate limits are scoped to `/api/*` paths only.
